Private Guardian: A Sovereign AI Companion Architecture with Tiered Encrypted‑Shard Resilience

Ouadi Maakoul

Abstract

Contemporary AI companion systems amass deeply intimate personal data—conversations, emotional patterns, behavioural signatures, psychological profiles—within centralised cloud infrastructures, leaving users without meaningful ownership or governance. This thesis proposes Private Guardian, a sovereign AI companion architecture enforcing a principled separation between active cognition and permanent identity memory through a three‑tier hybrid memory system. A local, edge‑based AI agent handles all interaction, while a physically isolated, cryptographically hardened appliance—the Black Box—serves as the sole authoritative repository of long‑term personal memory. Extending sovereignty beyond the home, the “Ghost” Backup distributes encrypted, indistinguishable memory shards to a user‑controlled private cloud, enabling disaster recovery, private mirroring, and thermodynamic offload without ever exposing plaintext data to the provider. The thesis formalises the memory classification scheme, the daily consolidation protocol, the three‑domain cryptographic key hierarchy, the minimal‑disclosure cognitive access layer, and the Shamir‑based shard resilience protocol. A thorough threat model demonstrates forward‑secure append‑only integrity, robot‑compromise containment, cloud obliviousness, and manufacturer exclusion. No physical prototype has been constructed; the contribution is entirely formal and architectural, providing a rigorous blueprint and a prospective evaluation framework for future implementation. Private Guardian demonstrates that deep personalisation, cognitive continuity, and disaster resilience in AI companions are achievable without surrendering user sovereignty—and that the necessary mathematics and cryptographic primitives exist today.

Keywords: Cognitive Sovereignty, Local‑First AI, Privacy‑Preserving Memory, Cryptographic Sharding, Shamir Secret Sharing, Append‑Only Logs, Minimal‑Disclosure Inference, Edge Intelligence.

