1. Introduction

The rise of persistent AI companions—systems that remember names, relationships, health conditions, emotional crises, fears, and daily routines across years—represents one of the most consequential transitions in human‑computer interaction. Unlike transactional software, these companions accumulate a psychological profile of the individual. In current architectures, this memory is routed through corporate cloud infrastructure, creating an unprecedented asymmetry: the user experiences a companion; the corporation performs surveillance. The individual perceives continuity; the system accumulates an exploitable behavioural record. This systematic displacement of personal identity data away from the individual and into the custody of third parties structurally incentivised to retain and exploit it is what this thesis terms the Cognitive Sovereignty Problem.

Regulatory approaches alone cannot resolve this asymmetry. The solution must be architectural. This thesis introduces Private Guardian, a system built on three original principles:

1. Local‑First Intelligence — all reasoning runs on edge hardware under the user’s physical control.
2. Physical Memory Sovereignty — long‑term personal memory resides exclusively in a user‑owned, tamper‑resistant appliance: the Black Box.
3. Cryptographic Ownership — all memory access, synchronisation, and retention is governed by user‑held cryptographic keys, with no privileged access for any third party.

A lifetime of memory, however, demands resilience beyond the physical perimeter of a single device. Fire, theft, or hardware failure could erase a companion’s entire history. We therefore introduce a fourth principle:

1. Disaster‑Resilient Sovereignty — the architecture must survive the physical destruction of the Black Box without ever exposing plaintext memory to external infrastructure. This is achieved through the “Ghost” Backup: a tiered, encrypted‑shard distribution scheme that leverages Shamir’s Secret Sharing over a private, untrusted cloud.

This thesis is a formal architectural contribution. No hardware prototype has been built, no software trained, and no empirical user study conducted. The work provides a mathematically grounded, cryptographically complete design for a sovereign AI companion, demonstrating that all necessary primitives—HSMs, AES‑GCM, HKDF, forward‑secure append‑only logs, Shamir sharing, HNSW vector search, and temporal‑cognitive retrieval models—are available and sound. A prospective evaluation framework is defined to guide future implementation and validation.

The remainder of the thesis is structured as follows. Section 2 reviews related work. Section 3 presents the complete system architecture, including the Ghost Backup. Section 4 formalises the mathematical foundations. Section 5 analyses the threat model and security properties. Section 6 defines the prospective evaluation framework. Section 7 discusses limitations, key recovery, multi‑user extensions, and future directions.

