2. Related Work

2.1 Centralised AI Companions and Surveillance Capitalism

Existing commercial AI companions follow a hub‑and‑spoke model: the user’s device acts as a thin client, and all inference, memory storage, and personalisation occur on remote servers. This architecture is operationally efficient but structurally incompatible with user sovereignty (Zuboff, 2019).

2.2 Local‑First Computing and Private Memory

Kleppmann et al. (2019) articulated the local‑first philosophy. Recent AI memory systems extend this vision: Engram provides an end‑to‑end encrypted, local‑first memory layer; SuperLocalMemory adds Bayesian trust scoring against memory poisoning; PARROT demonstrates a local‑first agentic pipeline with PII redaction. Opal (Kaviani et al., 2026) decouples data‑dependent reasoning from bulk personal data using trusted enclaves and ORAM—a closest relative, though it relies on software TEEs rather than physical isolation.

2.3 Retrieval‑Augmented Generation and Cognitive Memory

RAG (Lewis et al., 2020) enables language models to retrieve external context. Applied to companions, pure cosine‑similarity retrieval suffers from temporal blindness, frequency blindness, and importance blindness. SmartVector (Xu, 2026) and Neural Long Memory address these through multi‑signal scoring, achieving substantial accuracy gains. Private Guardian adopts a similar composite retrieval score tailored to sovereign memory categories.

2.4 Cryptographic Logging

Balloon (Pulls & Peeters, 2015) provides a forward‑secure, append‑only persistent authenticated data structure with history trees and hash treaps, enabling public verifiability. LogFAS (Yavuz et al., 2012) offers comparable properties, though Hartung (2017) demonstrated attacks on its security. Private Guardian employs a Balloon‑inspired hash chain suitable for single‑author, local‑verifier settings.

2.5 Secret Sharing and Disaster Recovery

Shamir’s (t,n) threshold scheme (Shamir, 1979) splits a secret into shares such that any t suffice for reconstruction while t-1 reveal nothing. Modern applications include Qasmat, which splits files into encrypted fragments distributed across multiple servers to ensure sovereignty without a single point of failure, and combinations with proxy re‑encryption for secure multi‑cloud storage.

2.6 Tiered Storage and Vector Search

Milvus and Qdrant implement tiered storage and HNSW indexing; Qdrant achieves 1.8 ms search latency at 10 million vectors, validating the performance targets assumed in our design.

2.7 Gap

No existing system integrates all five elements: local‑first AI companionship, physically isolated memory, cryptographic sovereignty, minimal‑disclosure access, and disaster‑resilient encrypted‑shard backup. Private Guardian does.

