5. Threat Model and Security Analysis

We consider an adversary with full network control, complete robot compromise, read access to all Ghost endpoints, and compromise of up to t-1 private‑cloud nodes simultaneously. Physical breach of the Black Box HSM and extraction of the master key are excluded.

Security properties formally proven or argued:

1. Robot Compromise Containment – exposure ≤ 24 hours of transient data; no long‑term memory or key material is accessible.
2. Network Isolation – offline default; Ghost transmits only indistinguishable encrypted blobs.
3. Log Tamper‑Evidence – Balloon hash chain detects any retroactive modification with probability 1-2^{-256}.
4. Category Compartmentalisation – HKDF guarantees computational independence of category keys.
5. Manufacturer Exclusion – no third party holds keys; firmware verified against user‑trusted certificates.
6. Disaster Resilience with Cloud Obliviousness – the cloud gains zero semantic information; Shamir secrecy ensures that even full cloud compromise reveals nothing without the master key.
7. Partial Cloud Compromise – any subset of fewer than 3 shares conveys zero information about the backup.

Limitations: side‑channel attacks on local inference, sensor poisoning, long‑term traffic analysis, and coercion scenarios are not formally modelled.

