7. Discussion

7.1 Key Recovery

The master key is split via a separate (t,n) Shamir scheme with shares distributed to trusted custodians and a biometric‑sealed enclave share within the Black Box. Ghost shards protect memory content only, not the master key; the two systems are complementary.

7.2 Multi‑User Household

The category hierarchy natively supports per‑user key domains; shared Family categories can be encrypted under a group key negotiated through a multi‑party key agreement. Full design remains future work.

7.3 Limitations

· No empirical validation: All performance figures are projections based on published benchmarks; real‑world behaviour under varied workloads, hardware, and attack simulations is unverified.
· Local LLM capability ceiling: Edge models currently trail cloud‑scale frontier models.
· Hardware cost: A FIPS 140‑3 Level 3 HSM adds significant expense.
· Post‑quantum resilience: Current constructions rely on classical assumptions.

7.4 Future Directions

· Implement a full prototype and measure all metrics defined in Section 6.
· Migrate from MDCA to true zero‑knowledge retrieval using VeriRAG/zkRAG as circuit efficiency matures.
· Extend to a Sovereign Cognitive Kernel, orchestrating identity and memory across multiple AI agents.
· Upgrade to post‑quantum threshold schemes and signature algorithms.

