Appendix A — Mathematical Foundations

A.1 Shamir’s Secret Sharing: Formal Construction and Secrecy Proof

Shamir’s (t,n) threshold scheme (Shamir, 1979) forms the cryptographic heart of the Ghost Backup. We present it in full formal detail.

Definition A.1 (Threshold Secret Sharing).
Let s \in \mathbb{F}_q be a secret taken from a finite field \mathbb{F}_q with q prime and q > n. A (t,n) threshold scheme is a pair of algorithms (\mathsf{Share}, \mathsf{Reconstruct}) such that:

1. \mathsf{Share}(s) \to \{s_1, \dots, s_n\}.
2. Any subset of t or more shares uniquely determines s.
3. Any subset of fewer than t shares reveals no information about s (perfect secrecy).

---

A.1.1 Share Algorithm

Select t-1 coefficients a_1, a_2, \dots, a_{t-1} uniformly at random from \mathbb{F}_q. Define the polynomial

f(x) = s + a_1 x + a_2 x^2 + \cdots + a_{t-1} x^{t-1} \pmod{q},

so that f(0) = s. Choose n distinct, non‑zero evaluation points x_1, \dots, x_n \in \mathbb{F}_q (e.g., the integers 1,2,\dots,n). Each share is the pair

s_i = (x_i, y_i = f(x_i)).

---

A.1.2 Reconstruct Algorithm (Lagrange Interpolation)

Given any t distinct shares \{(x_{i_j}, y_{i_j})\}_{j=1}^t, the polynomial f is uniquely reconstructed as

f(x) = \sum_{j=1}^{t} y_{i_j} \prod_{\substack{1 \leq k \leq t \\ k \neq j}} \frac{x - x_{i_k}}{x_{i_j} - x_{i_k}} \pmod{q}.

The secret is recovered by evaluating at zero:

s = f(0) = \sum_{j=1}^{t} y_{i_j} \prod_{\substack{1 \leq k \leq t \\ k \neq j}} \frac{x_{i_k}}{x_{i_k} - x_{i_j}} \pmod{q}.

---

A.1.3 Proof of Information‑Theoretic Secrecy

Let an adversary observe any set \mathcal{I} \subset \{1,\dots,n\} of size |\mathcal{I}| = t-1. For any candidate secret s' \in \mathbb{F}_q, there exists exactly one polynomial f' of degree \leq t-1 such that f'(0)=s' and f'(x_i) = y_i for all i \in \mathcal{I}. (The linear system defined by the Vandermonde matrix is fully determined once the constant term is fixed.) Since the coefficients a_1,\dots,a_{t-1} were chosen uniformly, the conditional distribution

\Pr[ s = s' \mid \{s_i\}_{i\in\mathcal{I}} ] = \frac{1}{q}

is uniform. Thus the adversary’s posterior belief equals the prior, guaranteeing zero information leakage. This property holds unconditionally, without any computational assumptions.

---

A.2 Cryptographic Key Hierarchy (HKDF, RFC 5869)

All keys in Private Guardian are derived from the master key \mathcal{K}_{\text{master}} using the HMAC‑based Extract‑and‑Expand Key Derivation Function (HKDF), standardised in RFC 5869 (Krawczyk, 2010).

Derivation Rule:

\mathcal{K}_{\text{domain}} = \text{HKDF}(\mathcal{K}_{\text{master}}, \text{salt}, \text{info} \| \text{version}),

where:

· \mathcal{K}_{\text{master}} is the 256‑bit master key, generated and permanently resident inside the Black Box HSM.
· \text{salt} is an optional non‑secret random value (may be zero for high‑entropy master keys).
· \text{info} encodes the target domain: "category:Medical", "ghost_backup", "transport:share:3", etc.
· \text{version} is a monotonic counter enabling key rotation.

HKDF’s formal guarantees (Krawczyk, 2010) ensure that the derived keys are computationally independent: knowledge of one domain key gives no practical advantage in computing another.

---

A.3 AES‑256‑GCM Encryption and Nonce Management

Memory entries and backup bundles are encrypted using AES‑256‑GCM (NIST SP 800‑38D). GCM provides both confidentiality and authenticated integrity via an authentication tag.

Nonce Derivation:
AES‑GCM demands a unique 96‑bit nonce for each encryption under a given key. Private Guardian derives the nonce from the entry’s monotonic position in the append‑only journal:

\text{nonce} = \text{encode\_uint96}(\text{sequence\_number}) \| \text{direction\_bit},

where direction_bit is 0 for robot→Black Box and 1 for Black Box→Ghost. Because the Black Box’s storage controller physically enforces append‑only writes, no sequence number is ever reused with the same key. This construction satisfies the uniqueness requirement unconditionally (cf. Libsodium documentation).

---

A.4 Append‑Only Log Integrity (Balloon Construction)

The Black Box’s journal is modelled on the Balloon forward‑secure append‑only persistent authenticated data structure (Pulls & Peeters, 2015). Each log entry is:

L_i = (E_i, h_i, \sigma_i, t_i),

with:

· E_i — encrypted memory payload,
· h_i = H(h_{i-1} \| E_i \| t_i \| i) where H = \text{SHA‑3‑256} and h_{-1} is a public genesis value,
· \sigma_i = \text{Sign}(\mathcal{K}_{\text{sign}}, h_i \| i) — Ed25519 signature using a per‑session robot signing key,
· t_i — RFC 3161 trusted timestamp.

Security Properties (proved in Pulls & Peeters, 2015):

· Forward security: No event inserted before a compromise of the author (the robot) can be modified or deleted undetectably.
· Tamper evidence: Any alteration of L_j for j \leq i breaks the hash chain with probability 1 - 2^{-256}.
· Public verifiability: The history tree structure enables efficient membership proofs; the hash treap enables non‑membership proofs.

---

A.5 Hybrid Memory Decay Model

Human forgetting follows an exponential trajectory over short intervals (Ebbinghaus, 1885) but transitions to a power law over long intervals (Wixted & Ebbesen, 1991; Wixted, 2004). Private Guardian uses a hybrid decay function:

\text{decay}(\Delta t) = 
\begin{cases}
e^{-\lambda_{\text{cat}} \Delta t}, & \Delta t \leq T_{\text{hot}} \\[4pt]
a \cdot (\Delta t)^{-b}, & \Delta t > T_{\text{hot}}
\end{cases}

Continuity at the crossover point T_{\text{hot}} is enforced by setting a = e^{-\lambda T_{\text{hot}}} \cdot T_{\text{hot}}^{\,b}. This hybrid formulation is computationally tractable and aligns with the empirical memory consolidation literature.

---

A.6 Multi‑Signal Retrieval Score

The retrieval ranking score for memory entry m_i given query q at time t is defined as:

\boxed{
\text{score}(m_i, q, t) = \alpha \cdot \text{cos}(\mathbf{q}, \mathbf{m}_i) 
+ \beta \cdot \text{decay}(t - t_i) 
+ \gamma \cdot \rho(m_i) 
+ \delta \cdot \frac{\log(1 + a_i)}{\log(1 + A_{\max})}
}

where:

· \alpha + \beta + \gamma + \delta = 1,
· \text{cos}(\mathbf{q}, \mathbf{m}_i) is the cosine similarity between query and memory embeddings,
· \rho(m_i) \in [0,1] is the memory’s original capture confidence,
· a_i is cumulative access count (retrieval reinforcement, Roediger & Karpicke, 2006),
· A_{\max} is a normalisation constant.

This composite score has been independently validated by the SmartVector (Xu, 2026) and Neural Long Memory frameworks, which demonstrate large accuracy gains over pure cosine retrieval.

